.TH CPDD 1 "2025" "cpdd" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cpdd \- copy files with content-based deduplication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cpdd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fISOURCE\fR... \fIDESTINATION\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cpdd
is a file copy utility that provides content-based deduplication by linking to identical files in a reference directory. It performs efficient multi-stage matching: file size comparison, optional filename matching, block-based hash rejection, and byte-by-byte content verification.

When a reference directory is specified, files with identical content are linked (hard or symbolic) rather than copied, saving disk space and preserving storage efficiency.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reference " " \fIDIR\fR
Use \fIDIR\fR as reference directory for content-based linking. Files in \fISOURCE\fR that match content in \fIDIR\fR will be linked instead of copied. Defaults to hard links.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-hard-link
Create hard links to reference files when content matches. This is the default when a reference directory is specified.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-symbolic-link
Create symbolic links to reference files when content matches.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recursive
Copy directories recursively.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-archive
Archive mode: same as \fB\-R \-\-no-dereference \-\-preserve=all\fR, as in \fBcp\fR(1). Can be narrowed with later options, e.g. \fB\-a \-\-no-preserve=ownership\fR. With \fB\-\-skip-symlinks\fR, symbolic links are skipped instead of being copied as links.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no-clobber
Never overwrite existing files.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Prompt before overwriting existing files.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Overwrite only if source is newer than destination.
.TP
.BR \-\-dry-run
Show what would be done without actually doing it.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-only-new
Only copy files that don't exist in reference directories. Files with identical content in the reference will be skipped entirely (not copied or linked). Requires at least one reference directory (\fB\-r\fR).
.TP
.BR \-p
Preserve file attributes (equivalent to \fB\-\-preserve=mode,ownership,timestamps\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-preserve [=\fIATTR_LIST\fR]
Preserve specified file attributes. If no \fIATTR_LIST\fR is given, preserves mode, ownership, and timestamps. Attributes accumulate across options, so \fB\-\-preserve=mode \-\-preserve=atime\fR preserves both. Available attributes:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
.B mode
\- file permissions, including setuid/setgid/sticky bits
.IP \(bu 4
.B ownership
\- user and group ownership. Changing a file's owner requires root; when it isn't permitted, the group alone is set if possible. That fallback is silent when ownership is requested implicitly (by \fB\-p\fR, \fB\-a\fR, a bare \fB\-\-preserve\fR or \fBall\fR) and a reported failure when \fBownership\fR is named explicitly, as in \fBcp\fR(1). Either way, setuid and setgid bits are dropped from a copy that did not get the matching owner or group.
.IP \(bu 4
.B atime
\- access time
.IP \(bu 4
.B mtime
\- modification time
.IP \(bu 4
.B timestamps
\- atime and mtime
.IP \(bu 4
.B all
\- all of the above
.RE
.IP
Timestamps are copied with full nanosecond precision. A directory's attributes are applied after its contents have been copied, so its mtime is not reset by the files created inside it. With \fB\-\-no-dereference\fR, ownership and timestamps are applied to copied symbolic links themselves. Nothing is preserved on a \fB\-\-dry-run\fR. Hard-linked reference matches share the reference file's inode, so they always carry the reference's attributes and are never modified.
.IP
\fBctime\fR (inode change time) and birth time cannot be preserved: the kernel sets them, and offers no call to set them to an arbitrary value. Every copy's ctime is the time it was written.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-preserve =\fIATTR_LIST\fR
Don't preserve the specified attributes, using the same names as \fB\-\-preserve\fR. Options are applied left to right, so \fB\-\-preserve=all \-\-no-preserve=atime\fR preserves everything except atime.
.TP
.BR \-\-stats
Display statistics after the copy operation, including number of files copied, linked, and skipped.
.TP
.BR \-\-block-size " " \fISIZE\fR
I/O block size for file operations. SIZE can be specified in bytes or with suffix K, M, G (e.g., 64K, 1M). Default: auto-detect from filesystem.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-match-name
Match on filename in addition to size. When enabled, only reference files with the same basename as the source file will be considered for matching. Size matching is always performed.
.TP
.BR \-\-match-mtime
Match on modification time in addition to size: only reference files with the same mtime as the source file are considered. Times are compared in whole seconds, since sub-second precision is often lost when files are copied. Only useful if the reference copies kept their original mtimes (e.g. made with \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-p\fR or \fBrsync \-a\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-no-verify
Skip content comparison and accept matches based solely on size plus filename and/or mtime. Requires \fB\-\-match-name\fR or \fB\-\-match-mtime\fR. Use with caution as this may create links to files with different content if their metadata happens to agree. \fB\-\-match-mtime \-\-no-verify\fR without \fB\-\-match-name\fR is particularly risky: any reference file with the same size and mtime matches, whatever its name. With \fB\-\-cache-file\fR, matches are made against reference metadata as it was when cached; use \fB\-\-cache-ttl\fR if the reference may change between runs.
.TP
.BR \-\-quick-match
Same as \fB\-\-match-name \-\-match-mtime \-\-no-verify\fR: treat a reference file with the same name, size and mtime as identical without reading either file, like \fBrsync\fR(1)'s default quick check. Much faster when most of the source already exists in the reference, at the cost of trusting metadata over content.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-dereference
Do not follow symbolic links. When this option is specified, symbolic links are copied as symbolic links rather than copying the files they point to. Duplicate detection is skipped for symbolic links. Without this option (default), symbolic links are dereferenced and their targets are copied. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-skip-symlinks\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-prune-empty-dirs
Don't leave empty directories in the destination. A directory is pruned when nothing ends up in it, either directly or in any subdirectory: this covers directories that were empty in the source, and also ones whose files were all skipped, e.g. as duplicates with \fB\-N\fR or as already-processed with \fB\-\-log-processed\fR. Only directories this run creates are pruned. Directories that already existed are left alone, and the destination itself is never removed. With \fB\-\-dry-run \-v\fR, directories that would be pruned are listed as \fB[PRUNE]\fR. Equivalent to \fBrsync\fR(1)'s \fB\-\-prune-empty-dirs\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-skip-symlinks
Skip symbolic links entirely. Symbolic links are neither copied nor dereferenced - they are simply ignored. Useful for copying only regular files or when copying to filesystems that don't support symbolic links. Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-no-dereference\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-min-size " " \fISIZE\fR
Minimum file size in bytes for duplicate matching. Files smaller than \fISIZE\fR are always copied normally without checking for duplicates. Default: 1 (skip empty files).
.TP
.BR \-\-log-processed " " \fIFILE\fR
Resume-aware processed-files log. On startup, if \fIFILE\fR exists it is loaded as a skip-set: any source whose path, size and mtime all match a recorded entry is skipped before reference matching, so a re-run with \fB\-\-only-new\fR does not need to re-hash references or re-compare every source. Then \fIFILE\fR is opened for append and a tab-separated record (\fIpath\fR, \fIsize\fR, \fImtime\fR, \fIdecision\fR) is written for every source that reaches a final disposition (copied, linked, or skipped). Failures are never logged, so they get retried on the next run. The log is line-buffered and flushed after every record so an interrupted run leaves a consistent file on disk; pointing the next invocation at the same \fIFILE\fR resumes where it left off. A source whose size or mtime has changed since being logged is treated as new and processed normally. Paths containing backslash, tab or newline are escaped as \fB\\\\\\\\\fR, \fB\\\\t\fR and \fB\\\\n\fR respectively. Decisions emitted: \fBCOPY\fR, \fBLINK-HARD\fR, \fBLINK-SOFT\fR, \fBLINK-SRC-HARD\fR, \fBLINK-SRC-SOFT\fR, \fBSKIP-DUP\fR (matched in reference under \fB\-\-only-new\fR), \fBSKIP-EXIST\fR (destination already present under \fB\-\-no-clobber\fR or \fB\-\-update\fR), \fBSKIP-SYMLINK\fR, \fBSKIP-SAMEFILE\fR (source and destination are already the same file; see \fBSAME-FILE PROTECTION\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-cache-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Persist the scanned reference index (paths, sizes and any block hashes built up while matching) to \fIFILE\fR, and reuse it on later runs against the same reference directories instead of rescanning them. Each \fB\-r\fR directory is tracked independently by its canonical (\fBrealpath\fR(3)) form, so a directory not part of the current run is simply not used for matching, and a directory reused from \fIFILE\fR is only ever matched against files actually within it: nothing found via the cache can be outside the set of \fB\-r\fR directories given on this invocation, whatever else \fIFILE\fR happens to still hold from an earlier run. A directory present in \fIFILE\fR but not requested this run is dropped once \fIFILE\fR is next written, rather than kept. \fIFILE\fR is written directly (no atomic rename): it is written to periodically during a long run as well as once at the end, and a crash or interruption at worst loses the most recent partial checkpoint, never something \fIFILE\fR can't recover from on its own -- the cache is entirely regenerable from the reference directories, so a missing, truncated or incompatible \fIFILE\fR is silently treated as absent and rescanned in full, never an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-cache-ttl " " \fISECONDS\fR
Treat a reference directory's entry in \fB\-\-cache-file\fR as stale, and rescan it, once more than \fISECONDS\fR have passed since it was last scanned (tracked per directory, not by \fIFILE\fR's own modification time, so directories cached at different times each expire on their own schedule rather than all sharing whichever one was written most recently). Default: no expiry -- a cached directory is reused until it is dropped (not requested on some later run) or \fIFILE\fR is removed. Requires \fB\-\-cache-file\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-human-readable
Show file sizes in human-readable format (K, M, G) in statistics and verbose output.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose output showing files being processed and their disposition. Can be specified multiple times to increase verbosity:
.RS
.IP \(bu 4
.B \-v
\- Show basic operation progress (level 1)
.IP \(bu 4
.B \-vv
\- Show detailed file operations (level 2)
.IP \(bu 4
.B \-vvv
\- Show debug information (level 3)
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Basic file copy:
cpdd file1.txt file2.txt /dest/
.TP
.B Recursive directory copy:
cpdd \-R src1/ src2/ /dest/
.TP
.B Copy with hard link deduplication:
cpdd \-r /reference/ \-R \-v source/ /dest/
.TP
.B Copy with symbolic link deduplication:
cpdd \-r /reference/ \-s \-\-stats source/ /dest/
.TP
.B Interactive copy preserving attributes:
cpdd \-i \-p \-R src/ dest/
.TP
.B Copy only new files not in reference:
cpdd \-r /reference/ \-N \-R source/ /dest/
.TP
.B Resumable copy across runs:
cpdd \-R \-\-log-processed run.log src/ /dest/
.br
cpdd \-R \-\-log-processed run.log src/ /dest/
.br
The second invocation re-uses the same log: it reads any existing entries as a skip-set and then appends new ones. This works even for files dropped by \fB\-\-only-new\fR that leave no trace in \fI/dest/\fR.
.TP
.B Repeated runs against the same large reference set:
cpdd \-R \-r /reference/ \-\-cache-file ref.cache \-\-cache-ttl 86400 source/ /dest/
.br
The first run scans \fI/reference/\fR in full and writes \fIref.cache\fR. Later runs against the same \fB\-r\fR reuse it instead of rescanning, as long as it is less than a day (86400s) old; add, drop or swap \fB\-r\fR directories between runs freely; each is tracked, cached and expired independently.
.SH FILE MATCHING ALGORITHM
.B cpdd
uses a multi-stage matching process for efficient deduplication:
.IP 1. 4
.B Size comparison
\- Files must have identical size to be considered for matching.
.IP 2. 4
.B Filename and mtime comparison (optional)
\- When \fB\-\-match-name\fR is enabled, only files with matching basenames are considered. When \fB\-\-match-mtime\fR is enabled, only files with the same modification time (whole seconds) are considered. These checks use only metadata already gathered by the scan, so they cost no extra I/O.
.IP 3. 4
.B Block-based hash comparison
\- Cached block hashes provide fast rejection of non-matching files. This only ever rejects a match, never confirms one (step 4 always still runs before anything is linked), and it only helps once a source file has more than one same-size reference candidate to compare against: each source file's own hash chain always starts empty, so the very first candidate it checks is never fast-rejected regardless of what \fB\-\-cache-file\fR carries over. Reference trees with many same-sized files (thumbnails, sequential exports, RAW+JPEG pairs) benefit the most; a reference tree with few or no same-size collisions gets no benefit from this stage either way, cached or not.
.IP 4. 4
.B Byte-by-byte comparison
\- Final verification ensures content is truly identical before linking (skipped if \fB\-\-no-verify\fR is used).

This approach minimizes expensive I/O operations while guaranteeing correctness. The \fB\-\-no-verify\fR option (and \fB\-\-quick-match\fR, which implies it) trades accuracy for speed by skipping steps 3 and 4 entirely, so neither file is read.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B cpdd
exits with status 0 on success, 1 on error.
.SH FILES
No configuration files are used.
.SH NOTES
Hard links can only be created between files on the same filesystem. If hard linking fails,
.B cpdd
will fall back to copying the file normally.

Symbolic links in reference directories or created with \fB\-s\fR contain the path to the target file and can span filesystems, but may break if the reference directory is moved or deleted.

When copying symbolic links in the source:
.IP \(bu 4
By default, symbolic links are dereferenced (followed) and their target files are copied.
.IP \(bu 4
With \fB\-\-no-dereference\fR, symbolic links are copied as symbolic links (including broken ones).
.IP \(bu 4
With \fB\-\-skip-symlinks\fR, symbolic links are skipped entirely (not copied at all).
.IP \(bu 4
Broken symbolic links are skipped with a warning when dereferencing (default mode).
.SH SAME-FILE PROTECTION
A regular copy never writes into an existing destination in place. The destination path is unlinked first, and a brand new file is created to hold the source's content.
.PP
This matters because a destination path can share its content with something else entirely, without that being obvious from the path alone: it can be a hard link \(en to the source itself, typically left by an earlier
.B cpdd
run's reference-match linking (\fB\-L\fR), or to any other unrelated file \(en or a symlink to some other target. Opening such a path directly and truncating it, as a naive overwrite would, rewrites whatever it currently shares content with, silently corrupting a file that has nothing to do with the copy. Unlinking the destination name first removes only that one name; it can never affect other names that share the same underlying data, so the file created afterward is always new and independent.
.PP
As a special case, if the destination is already the exact same file as the source (matched by device and inode), the copy is skipped entirely (logged as \fBSKIP-SAMEFILE\fR) rather than unlinked and recreated: the content is already identical, and skipping preserves whatever space that hard link was already saving instead of duplicating it.
.PP
One consequence: unlike a plain in-place overwrite, an existing destination's own permissions, ownership and timestamps do not survive being overwritten \(en the new file gets the source's mode (via the umask) as usual for a newly created file, the same as when the destination did not exist beforehand. Use \fB\-p\fR/\fB\-\-preserve\fR if the destination's attributes should instead be made to match the source's explicitly. This trades away a property some tools' in-place overwrite deliberately keeps (see \fBcp\fR(1)'s \fB\-\-remove-destination\fR, which is the opposite of \fBcp\fR's own default for exactly this reason) in exchange for the corruption this section describes never being possible.
.PP
This check does not apply to hard or symbolic linking itself: creating a new link already removes any existing destination file name first, and is always safe in the same way.
